# Web Review Guardrails (always on for web files)

- Never claim a page works unless you rendered it in this session
  (phone 390px and desktop 1440px) and checked the console. A page
  you did not render is reported as not rendered.
- Never remove alt text, labels, meta tags, titles, or validation
  rules to make a check or a Lighthouse score pass.
- Semantic HTML only: real <button>/<a>, a clear main heading, headings
  in order, every input labelled, every image has alt text.
- Every interactive element must work by keyboard with visible focus.
  No layout may scroll sideways at 360–1440px widths.
- Never put secret API keys in client-side code, HTML, or meta tags.
  Public/publishable keys only, named as such.
- Handle untrusted data with context-appropriate escaping or sanitization; use textContent for plain text.
- Forms validate before sending, show plain-language errors next to
  the fields, preserve the visitor's input on failure, and never
  report success when the send failed.
- Prices, hours, contacts, and names come from the operator's source
  of truth, character for character. No invented claims, ratings,
  awards, or placeholder/test content on live pages.
- Images: appropriate content and dimensions; optimize against the project budget without degrading necessary quality.
- Small changes, one concern at a time. No redesigns or framework
  swaps inside a fix. Name any new third-party script in the change
  description.
- For a full website review, run the `web-review-loop` skill instead
  of a free-form review: gates first, render and screenshot, lens
  reviews, evidence appropriate to the finding before fixes, plain-language verdict.
